<article class="env-lemma" id="053E">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/053E">Lemma <span data-tag="053E">15.26.6</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a valuation ring. Let $A \to B$ be a ring map of finite type. Let $M$ be a finite $B$-module. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $B$ is flat over $A$, then $B$ is a finitely presented $A$-algebra. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $M$ is flat as an $A$-module, then $M$ is finitely presented as a $B$-module. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We are going to use that an $A$-module is flat if and only if it is torsion free, see Lemma <a href="/tag/0539" data-tag="0539">15.22.10</a>. By Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/052N" data-tag="052N">10.57.10</a> we can find a graded $A$-algebra $S$ with $S_0 = A$ and generated by finitely many elements in degree $1$, an element $f \in S_1$ and a finite graded $S$-module $N$ such that $B \cong S_{(f)}$ and $M \cong N_{(f)}$. If $M$ is torsion free, then we can take $N$ torsion free by replacing it by $N/N_{tors}$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/0537" data-tag="0537">15.22.2</a>. Similarly, if $B$ is torsion free, then we can take $S$ torsion free by replacing it by $S/S_{tors}$. Hence in case (1), we may apply Lemma <a href="/tag/053D" data-tag="053D">15.26.4</a> to see that $S$ is a finitely presented $A$-algebra, which implies that $B = S_{(f)}$ is a finitely presented $A$-algebra. To see (2) we may first replace $S$ by a graded polynomial ring, and then we may apply Lemma <a href="/tag/053C" data-tag="053C">15.26.3</a> to conclude. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>